          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why does acid reflux tend to happen after meals?",
          "answer": "Acid reflux, also known as gastroesophageal reflux, often occurs after meals due to several factors. When you eat, your stomach produces acid to help digest food. If the lower esophageal sphincter (the muscle that separates the stomach from the esophagus) is weak or relaxes improperly, it can allow stomach acid to escape into the esophagus. \n\nAdditionally, the type and amount of food consumed can influence reflux. Foods that are spicy, fatty, or acidic may worsen the symptoms. Eating large meals can also increase pressure on the lower esophageal sphincter, leading to reflux.\n\nI cannot cover detailed mechanisms of how different foods cause acid reflux or individual variations in symptoms. For personalized guidance and treatment options, discussing with a healthcare provider is essential.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
